###  http://w3id.org/aqfo/aqfo_00002182
<http://w3id.org/aqfo/aqfo_00002182> rdf:type owl:Class ;
                                     rdfs:subClassOf <http://w3id.org/aqfo/aqfo_00002181> ;
                                     obo:IAO_0000115 "Ridge or hummock formed in shallow ocean areas from the external skeletons of corals. The skeleton consists of calcium carbonate (CaCO3), or limestone. A coral reef may grow into a permanent coral island, or it may take one of four principal forms. Fringing reefs consist of a flat reef area around a nonreef island. Barrier reefs may lie a mile or more offshore, separated from the landmass by a lagoon or channel. Atolls are circular reefs without a central landmass. Patch reefs have irregular tablelike or pinnacle features. Smaller patches occur inside atoll lagoons; larger patches occur as isolated parts of any of the other three reef categories, and they sometimes occur completely separate from other kinds of reefs." ;
                                     dc:contributor <http://orcid.org/0000-0002-8461-9745> ;
                                     rdfs:label "coral reef" .

[ rdf:type owl:Axiom ;
   owl:annotatedSource <http://w3id.org/aqfo/aqfo_00002182> ;
   owl:annotatedProperty obo:IAO_0000115 ;
   owl:annotatedTarget "Ridge or hummock formed in shallow ocean areas from the external skeletons of corals. The skeleton consists of calcium carbonate (CaCO3), or limestone. A coral reef may grow into a permanent coral island, or it may take one of four principal forms. Fringing reefs consist of a flat reef area around a nonreef island. Barrier reefs may lie a mile or more offshore, separated from the landmass by a lagoon or channel. Atolls are circular reefs without a central landmass. Patch reefs have irregular tablelike or pinnacle features. Smaller patches occur inside atoll lagoons; larger patches occur as isolated parts of any of the other three reef categories, and they sometimes occur completely separate from other kinds of reefs." ;
   oboInOwl:hasDbXref "The Editors of Encyclopaedia Britannica. (2020, Jun 18). Coral reef summary. Encyclopedia Britannica. ;https://www.britannica.com/summary/coral-reef"
 ] .


###  http://w3id.org/aqfo/aqfo_00002183
